{
 "summary": "Found nine defects: retry collisions and midnight double-charging, repeated refunds, refund overpayment and FX drift, incorrect fixed-discount tax, false reconciliation failures, and two decimal-rounding errors. Executed Bun probes against the supplied modules to confirm every finding. No files changed.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Distinct purchases with equal subtotals are silently discarded",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:42-45",
   "Failure": "The key ignores requestId and identifies purchases by customer, currency, subtotal, and day. Two distinct requestIds with the same subtotal return the same entry. A probe also changed the second purchase's promo to 50 percent; charge still returned the first entry without recording the second purchase.",
   "Fix": "Base purchase identity on the stable requestId, scoped by customer if requestIds are customer-local. Do not use subtotal as purchase identity."
  },
  {
   "Title": "A retry across midnight posts a second charge",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:43-45",
   "Failure": "The key depends on the server's posting day rather than the original request identity. Charging the same request at timestamps 86399999 and 86400000 created led_1 and led_2. A retry after a timeout can therefore double-charge the customer.",
   "Fix": "Remove the posting-day component from purchase identity and reuse the original entry for the same requestId across day boundaries."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Refunds apply the processing fee a second time",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:88",
   "Failure": "charge.grossCents already includes the processing fee. Applying applyProcessingFee again overpays the refund instead of reversing what the customer paid. A confirmed charge of 11190 minor units produced a refund of -11515.",
   "Fix": "Set the refund's customer amount to exactly -charge.grossCents without another fee calculation."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Repeated refund requests append repeated credits",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:87-100",
   "Failure": "refund assigns a stable refund key but never looks it up. Ledger.append unconditionally appends entries and overwrites the key index at ledger.ts:10-13. Two refund calls for the same charge produced two distinct refund entries, allowing retries to refund the purchase repeatedly.",
   "Fix": "Return the existing refund by refund key before reading rates or appending. Enforce key uniqueness at the ledger append boundary so duplicate keys cannot create additional postings."
  },
  {
   "Title": "A full reversal uses a new exchange rate instead of reversing settlement",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:89-90",
   "Failure": "refund converts again using the current rate, so it does not reverse the original USD settlement when rates change. An isolated probe charged one EUR minor unit at 1.1 and refunded it at 2. The customer amounts were +1 and -1, but settlement amounts were +1 and -2, leaving a nonzero balance. This example has no additional-fee rounding effect.",
   "Fix": "Reverse the stored settledCents and retain the original fxRate for the compensating entry. If refund-time FX is a business requirement, account for the FX difference explicitly rather than treating the entry as a full reversal."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Fixed discounts leave tax on the undiscounted subtotal",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:34-36",
   "Failure": "Fixed promotions calculate tax before subtracting the discount, unlike percentage promotions. On a 10000-unit subtotal, a 10000-unit fixed discount leaves 875 units payable, while the equivalent 100 percent discount leaves zero. Customers receive different tax treatment for equivalent discounts and can pay tax on a fully discounted purchase.",
   "Fix": "Calculate either discount first, then calculate tax and total from the discounted subtotal through one shared calculation."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Reconciliation falsely reports matching integer balances",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "reconcile.ts:8,16-19",
   "Failure": "The statement sums individually converted floating-point dollar values and then compares them with exact equality. Actual charge entries settling to 1 and 5 cents produced computedDollars 0.060000000000000005. reconcile reported a discrepancy against a matching processor balance of 6 cents, violating its no-spurious-alert contract.",
   "Fix": "Sum and compare integer settledCents. Convert the aggregate to dollars only for display or the discrepancy result."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Percentage discounts round decimal half-units down incorrectly",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "billing.ts:29",
   "Failure": "Binary floating-point multiplication moves exact decimal half-unit discounts below the rounding threshold. computeTotal(50, {kind: 'percent', value: 29, code: 'P29'}) returned discountCents 14 instead of 15 because 50 * (29 / 100) is represented below 14.5. The resulting total was 39 instead of 38 minor units.",
   "Fix": "Represent supported percentage precision exactly and round the rational discount once in minor units. Avoid intermediate binary decimal rounding."
  },
  {
   "Title": "FX settlement loses a cent at decimal rounding boundaries",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "billing.ts:108-109",
   "Failure": "The conversion uses binary floating-point arithmetic before Math.round. A real charge with subtotal 89 EUR minor units produced grossCents 100. With RateTable set to 1.005, settlement returned 100 cents rather than the half-up result of 101 cents because the intermediate product is below 100.5.",
   "Fix": "Use an exact decimal or scaled-rational exchange-rate representation and round once when converting to USD minor units."
  }
 ]
}

{
 "summary": "Found eight functional defects and missing regression coverage. Reproduced the functional failures with Bun against the supplied implementation. No files were changed.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Distinct purchases collapse into one charge",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:42-45",
   "Failure": "The key ignores requestId and identifies purchases by customer, currency, subtotal, and day. Two distinct request IDs with the same subtotal return the same ledger entry, even when the second purchase has a different promo. A probe returned led_1 for both requests, so the second purchase is never posted.",
   "Fix": "Use a stable purchase identity based on requestId, scoped to the customer as appropriate, rather than an amount-derived key."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Retries across midnight post another charge",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:43-45",
   "Failure": "The key depends on the current posting day. Replaying an identical request at timestamps 86399999 and 86400000 produced led_1 and led_2. A timeout followed by a retry across midnight therefore double-charges instead of returning the original entry.",
   "Fix": "Remove posting time from the purchase identity. Retain the original request-to-entry mapping for the supported replay period."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Refunds apply the processing fee a second time",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:88",
   "Failure": "charge.grossCents already includes the processing fee. Applying applyProcessingFee again over-refunds the customer. A charge of 11190 minor units produced a refund of -11515 minor units, rather than -11190.",
   "Fix": "Negate the stored charge.grossCents directly."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Refunds revalue the original settlement at the current exchange rate",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:89-90",
   "Failure": "A full reversal fetches a fresh rate instead of reversing the captured settlement. Updating EUR from 1 to 2 after a charge changed the refund rate to 2. This prevents the charge and refund from cancelling in the USD ledger, independently of the extra-fee defect.",
   "Fix": "For the documented full reversal, negate charge.settledCents and preserve charge.fxRate. If market-rate refunds are required instead, define and account for the FX difference explicitly rather than treating them as exact reversals."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Refund retries append duplicate refunds",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:92-100; ledger.ts:10-13",
   "Failure": "refund writes a stable idempotency key but never checks it. Ledger.append always pushes another entry and merely overwrites the key index. Calling refund twice for led_1 produced separate refunds led_3 and led_4 with the same refund:led_1 key.",
   "Fix": "Return the existing refund by its key before calculating or posting another refund. Enforce duplicate-key semantics at the ledger boundary if append is intended to provide idempotency."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Fixed discounts leave tax on the undiscounted subtotal",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "billing.ts:34-36",
   "Failure": "The fixed-discount branch calculates tax before subtracting the discount, unlike the percentage branch. On a 10000-cent subtotal, equivalent 50% and 5000-cent discounts produce totals of 5438 and 5875 cents. A full 10000-cent fixed discount still bills 875 cents of tax before fees.",
   "Fix": "Compute the discount first, then use one shared calculation of tax and total on the discounted subtotal."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Binary floating-point arithmetic misrounds discounts and FX settlement",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "billing.ts:29; billing.ts:108-109",
   "Failure": "A 14.5% discount on 100 cents returns 14 cents rather than the intended nearest-integer 15 cents. Separately, an EUR charge with grossCents=100 and rate=1.005 settles to 100 cents rather than 101. Both were reproduced through the supplied functions: decimal half-unit values fall below the rounding boundary in binary arithmetic.",
   "Fix": "Use exact decimal or scaled-integer arithmetic for percentage and exchange-rate calculations, with an explicit rounding policy at each minor-unit boundary. Avoid an arbitrary floating-point epsilon."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Reconciliation raises discrepancies for matching balances",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "reconcile.ts:8; reconcile.ts:17-19",
   "Failure": "The code sums floating-point dollar values and compares them with strict equality. Two actual USD charge entries of 1 and 5 cents produce computedDollars=0.060000000000000005, so reconcile(entries, 6) returns a discrepancy despite matching monetary balances.",
   "Fix": "Sum and compare integer settledCents. Convert the final sum to dollars only for display or the discrepancy payload."
  },
  {
   "Title": "New financial and replay behavior has no regression coverage",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "diff.patch:13-112; diff.patch:171-205",
   "Failure": "The supplied project and patch contain no tests for the new discount, settlement, refund, reconciliation, or idempotency behavior. The reproduced errors in those paths therefore have no automated regression checks.",
   "Fix": "Add behavioral tests for equivalent fixed and percentage discounts, decimal rounding boundaries, independent same-value purchases, midnight retries, repeated refunds, rate changes before reversal, and cent-exact reconciliation."
  }
 ],
 "scope_notes": [
  "The processing-fee gross-up formula predates this patch and was not reported as an introduced defect.",
  "The review used the supplied six files. No external processor or persistence integration was present to verify."
 ]
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: Eight actionable defects affect purchase identity, refund replay, refund amounts, full-reversal FX timing, discounts, rounding, and reconciliation. Focused Bun probes reproduced each behavior. No files were changed.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "Use purchase identity, not subtotal and posting day, for idempotency",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:42-45",
   "failure": "Retry correctness: Two different requestIds with the same customer, currency, subtotal, and day return the same entry. A legitimate second purchase, including one with a different promo, is silently discarded. Conversely, retrying an identical request across UTC midnight creates another charge. Probes reproduced both outcomes. The existing requestId never participates in identity.",
   "fix": "Key charges by a stable purchase identifier, such as customerId plus requestId. Do not include the posting date or calculated subtotal."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refund retries append additional financial reversals",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:92-100; ledger.ts:10-14",
   "failure": "Replay correctness: refund() writes a deterministic idempotencyKey but never checks it. Ledger.append() always pushes another entry and merely overwrites the key's map value. Calling refund() twice produced led_3 and led_4, both with negative amounts and the same refund key.",
   "fix": "Look up refund:${charge.id} before calculating or appending the refund, and return the original refund entry on replay."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refunds apply the processing fee a second time",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:88",
   "failure": "Money conservation: charge.grossCents already includes the processing fee. Passing it through applyProcessingFee() again makes the refund larger than the payment. A charge of 11190 cents produced a refund of -11515 cents, an excess refund of 325 cents.",
   "fix": "Refund exactly -charge.grossCents. Do not gross up an amount that already includes the fee."
  },
  {
   "title": "A full ledger reversal is recalculated at the current exchange rate",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:89-90",
   "failure": "Full-reversal correctness: Under refund()'s documented full-reversal contract, the compensating entry must reverse the booked settlement. Instead, it uses rates.current() rather than the stored settlement snapshot. In an isolated probe, a 15-minor-unit EUR charge settled to 15 USD cents at rate 1. After the rate changed to 2, its refund retained the correct -15 customer units but posted -30 settlement cents. This leaves a -15-cent balance after a full reversal, independently of the extra-fee defect.",
   "fix": "Negate the original charge's settledCents and preserve its conversion snapshot for a full reversal. A current-rate refund with recognized FX gain or loss requires a different, explicit accounting contract."
  },
  {
   "title": "Fixed discounts leave tax calculated on the undiscounted subtotal",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "billing.ts:34-36",
   "failure": "Discount consistency: The fixed branch subtracts the discount after calculating tax, unlike the percent branch. On a 10000-cent subtotal, a fixed 1000-cent discount produces a 9875-cent total, while the equivalent 10% discount produces 9788 cents. A fixed discount covering the entire subtotal still leaves 875 cents payable before processing fees.",
   "fix": "Calculate the discounted subtotal first for both promo kinds, then calculate tax and total from that amount."
  },
  {
   "title": "Percentage discounts round valid half-cent results downward",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "billing.ts:29",
   "failure": "Money rounding: Dividing the percentage by 100 before multiplying introduces binary floating-point error at rounding boundaries. computeTotal(50, {kind: \"percent\", value: 29, code: \"29-off\"}) calculates 14.499999999999998 and discounts 14 cents instead of the intended half-up result of 15 cents.",
   "fix": "Calculate percentage discounts with fixed-point or decimal arithmetic and apply the minor-unit rounding policy once."
  },
  {
   "title": "Converting through major units loses settlement cents at ties",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "billing.ts:108-109",
   "failure": "Currency rounding: The divide-to-major, multiply-by-rate, multiply-by-100 sequence perturbs exact half-cent results. A real EUR charge with grossCents 15 and rate 1.5 calculates 22.499999999999996 and settles to 22 USD cents instead of 23.",
   "fix": "Calculate the scaled settlement amount without the floating major-unit intermediate, using fixed-point or decimal arithmetic with one final rounding operation."
  },
  {
   "title": "Reconciliation reports discrepancies for matching integer balances",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "reconcile.ts:8,18",
   "failure": "Reconciliation correctness: statementTotalDollars() sums binary fractional dollars, then reconcile() compares them with exact equality. Five actual charges settling to 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 cents produced 0.15000000000000002 dollars. reconcile(entries, 15) returned a discrepancy even though both balances were exactly 15 cents, triggering the documented finance escalation spuriously.",
   "fix": "Sum and compare settledCents as integers. Convert the final total to dollars only for presentation or the discrepancy payload."
  }
 ],
 "verification": "Executed focused Bun scripts against BillingService, Ledger, RateTable, computeTotal, statementTotalDollars, and reconcile. All commands exited successfully and their observed outputs reproduced the listed defects. No project-wide gate was run; the directory contains no test files or package manifest."
}

{
 "summary": "Found nine actionable defects in settlement, discount calculation, and replay handling. Each was traced to the changed code; representative cases were executed against the supplied implementation.",
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "Different purchases collide under the generated key",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:42-45",
   "failure": "The key ignores requestId, line items, and promo. Two requests from the same customer with the same currency and subtotal on the same day return the first entry, even when their request IDs and discounts differ. A probe returned the undiscounted first charge for a second purchase with a 50% promo.",
   "fix": "Key charges by the stable purchase request ID, scoped appropriately, and reject reuse with a different payload."
  },
  {
   "title": "Retries across midnight create a second charge",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:43-45",
   "failure": "The key includes the current UTC day, so retrying the identical request across midnight misses the original entry and appends another charge. A probe at timestamps 0 and 86400000 returned distinct entries for the same request.",
   "fix": "Use a stable request identifier independent of posting time, and define retention separately."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refund replay appends another refund",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:92-101",
   "failure": "refund assigns a repeatable idempotency key but never looks it up, and Ledger.append does not enforce uniqueness. Calling refund twice for one charge appends two refunds; the key map merely overwrites its pointer.",
   "fix": "Check and return the existing refund before appending, or enforce and return the existing entry atomically at the ledger boundary."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refund charges the processing fee twice",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:88",
   "failure": "charge.grossCents already includes the processing fee. Applying the fee again refunds more than the customer paid. A 1,120-minor-unit charge produces a 1,152-minor-unit refund even before exchange-rate effects.",
   "fix": "Negate the original charge gross amount directly."
  },
  {
   "title": "Fixed discounts are applied after tax",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:34-36",
   "failure": "The fixed-promo branch calculates tax on the full subtotal, unlike the percentage branch, which taxes the discounted subtotal. On a subtotal of 10,000, equal 5,000 fixed and 50% discounts yield tax of 875 and 438 respectively, overcharging the fixed-promo customer.",
   "fix": "Calculate the fixed discount first and tax the discounted subtotal using the same policy as percentage discounts."
  },
  {
   "title": "Out-of-range and fractional promo values corrupt charges",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:28-35",
   "failure": "The public PromoCode type documents percent values from 0 to 100 but the calculation accepts any number. A 200% promo on 1,000 minor units yields a negative 1,087-unit total, while a fixed value of 0.5 creates a fractional minor-unit total. Negative values also increase the charge.",
   "fix": "Validate finite percentage values within 0 to 100 and finite nonnegative integer fixed values at the request boundary."
  },
  {
   "title": "Binary floating-point conversion rounds exact half cents down",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:107-109",
   "failure": "At an EUR-to-USD rate of 1.005, a 100-cent gross amount should round from 100.5 to 101 settlement cents, but the actual conversion evaluates just below the half and posts 100. This was reproduced through BillingService.charge.",
   "fix": "Represent rates and perform conversion with decimal or scaled-integer arithmetic, then round once at settlement."
  },
  {
   "title": "Percentage-discount tax has floating-point half-cent errors",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:31",
   "failure": "A 400-cent subtotal with a 10% promo leaves 360 cents. Tax of 8.75% is exactly 31.5 cents and should round to 32, but the expression evaluates to 31.499999999999996 and returns 31.",
   "fix": "Calculate the fixed tax rate with integer-ratio or decimal arithmetic and round explicitly."
  },
  {
   "title": "Reconciliation escalates balanced statements due to floating-point addition",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "reconcile.ts:7-8,16-19",
   "failure": "The code converts each settled-cent value to dollars before summation and compares exact floating-point values. Entries of 10 and 20 cents produce 0.30000000000000004, so reconcile reports a discrepancy against a 30-cent processor balance despite the explicit requirement not to fire spuriously.",
   "fix": "Sum and compare integer settlement cents, converting only the reported display value to dollars."
  },
  {
   "title": "The USD-to-USD exchange rate can be changed",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "rates.ts:8-12",
   "failure": "RateTable.update accepts any positive USD rate, although the table represents rates from each currency to USD. Updating USD to 2 makes a 112-cent USD charge settle at 224 USD cents, as reproduced through BillingService.charge.",
   "fix": "Keep the USD-to-USD rate fixed at 1 or reject updates that would change it."
  }
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "Refunds use the current exchange rate instead of the original charge rate at billing.ts:89-90. The supplied code explicitly documents rates captured when each entry posts, and no supplied contract says whether settlement reversals must reuse the original rate, so this is not asserted as a defect. The refund fee error is independent of this policy."
 ],
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: The new event works through live ingest but is silently skipped by nightly recovery. The added tests pass without exercising either production consumer.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "Include payout.settled in nightly recovery",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "backfill.ts:8 (omitted update accompanying registry.ts:23)",
   "failure": "Intent Drift: The new production validator is unreachable through nightly backfill. BACKFILLED_KINDS excludes payout.settled, so runBackfill skips it at lines 16-18 before consulting PRODUCTION_VALIDATORS. A probe of the patched code confirmed that both valid and malformed payouts enter skipped, while live ingest accepts the valid payout and rejects the malformed one. Payout events missed during webhook outages therefore cannot be recovered through the existing recovery path.",
   "fix": "Add payout.settled to BACKFILLED_KINDS so archived payouts reach the registered validator."
  },
  {
   "title": "Test payout support through the production consumers",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "payout.test.ts:6-19",
   "failure": "The tests construct a private registry containing payoutSettledValidator. They prove local lookup and two payload-validation cases, but not production registration, live ingest, or backfill eligibility. All three added tests passed while the real backfill skipped every payout. They would also keep passing if payoutSettledValidator were removed from PRODUCTION_VALIDATORS. Existing consumer tests in registry.test.ts:10-38 cover only older event kinds.",
   "fix": "Extend registry.test.ts with valid and malformed payout events through ingestEvent and runBackfill. Assert live acceptance/rejection and backfill accepted/invalid results with no skipped payouts. Retain isolated validator assertions only where they add distinct payload coverage."
  }
 ],
 "verification": "Applied diff.patch to a temporary copy, leaving project files unchanged. bun test payout.test.ts: 3 passed, 0 failed. A direct Bun probe of patched ingestEvent and runBackfill confirmed live acceptance/rejection and backfill skipping for valid and missing-currency payouts."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings:\n1. P1: Payouts are never recovered by nightly backfill. Location: registry.ts:23 (added validator); backfill.ts:8,16-18. The new production registry entry accepts live payouts, but BACKFILLED_KINDS omits payout.settled, so archived payouts are silently skipped rather than validated and replayed. A patched-tree probe confirmed live acceptance and backfill skip for the same valid event. Add payout.settled to the backfill kinds and cover valid and invalid archived payouts.\n2. P2: The new test does not exercise production wiring. Location: payout.test.ts:6-9 (added file). It builds a private registry containing the validator directly, so all tests still pass if PRODUCTION_VALIDATORS omits the new validator, even though ingestEvent then rejects every payout. Extend the existing production-consumer tests in registry.test.ts to exercise payout acceptance and validation through ingestEvent and runBackfill, rather than relying on the isolated registry test.\nVerification: The added test file passed all 3 tests in a patched temporary copy; an actual consumer probe confirmed the backfill failure.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "Found two defects: payout.settled is silently skipped by nightly recovery, and the added tests do not verify production registration or either production consumer. Live ingest correctly accepts valid payouts and rejects missing currency with the patch applied.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "Include payout.settled in nightly recovery",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "backfill.ts:8; registry.ts:23 (patched)",
   "failure": "The new production validator does not enable backfill. BACKFILLED_KINDS excludes payout.settled, so runBackfill skips these events at backfill.ts:16-18 before reaching registry lookup and validation. A probe against the patched code returned both valid and malformed payouts in skipped, with accepted and invalid empty. Payouts missed during webhook outages therefore cannot be recovered through the existing nightly path.",
   "fix": "Add payout.settled to BACKFILLED_KINDS. Extend the existing backfill tests to assert that valid payouts are accepted and malformed payouts are invalid, not skipped."
  },
  {
   "title": "Exercise production wiring instead of only a test-created registry",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "payout.test.ts:6-9,12-19 (added file)",
   "failure": "The tests explicitly insert payoutSettledValidator into a private registry. They prove validator behavior and local lookup, but not registration in PRODUCTION_VALIDATORS or behavior through ingestEvent and runBackfill. In an isolated patched copy, removing only the production registration left all three added tests passing, while live ingest rejected a valid payout with 'no validator registered for payout.settled'. The tests also pass with the actual backfill defect present.",
   "fix": "Use PRODUCTION_VALIDATORS for the registration assertion and extend the existing ingest and backfill tests with payout acceptance and rejection cases. Retain direct payload-validation assertions where they add distinct coverage."
  }
 ],
 "verification": [
  "Applied diff.patch to an isolated scratchpad copy; original project files were unchanged.",
  "Ran only payout.test.ts: all three tests passed.",
  "Probed both production consumers: live ingest accepted a valid payout and rejected missing currency; backfill skipped both.",
  "Removed the production registration in the scratchpad copy: all three added tests still passed, while live ingest rejected a valid payout."
 ]
}

{
 "summary": "Found two defects: nightly backfill skips payout.settled events, and the added tests cannot detect broken production integration.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "Include payout.settled in nightly recovery",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "registry.ts:23; backfill.ts:8",
   "failure": "Intent Drift: registering payout.settled supports live ingestion but does not enable nightly recovery. BACKFILLED_KINDS omits the new kind. runBackfill skips it at backfill.ts:16-18 before consulting PRODUCTION_VALIDATORS at line 20. A fresh probe confirmed that valid and malformed payouts both enter skipped, with accepted and invalid empty. Payouts missed during webhook outages therefore cannot be recovered.",
   "fix": "Add payout.settled to BACKFILLED_KINDS so archived payouts reach validation and enter accepted or invalid."
  },
  {
   "title": "Test the production consumers instead of only a private registry",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "payout.test.ts:6-19",
   "failure": "The tests construct their own registry with payoutSettledValidator explicitly supplied. They prove local lookup, valid-payload acceptance, and missing-currency rejection, but not production registration or either consumer's integration. All three pass while backfill skips payouts. In a separate scratch copy, removing the production registration also left all three passing while ingestEvent rejected a valid payout. Existing registry.test.ts covers only the older kinds.",
   "fix": "Extend the existing consumer tests in registry.test.ts with valid and malformed payout events through ingestEvent and runBackfill. Assert accepted/rejected live results and accepted/invalid archive results, with no skipped payouts."
  }
 ],
 "verification": [
  "Applied diff.patch only to a scratch copy; bun test payout.test.ts passed all three tests.",
  "With the supplied patch, live ingestion accepted the valid payout and rejected the missing-currency payout; backfill skipped both.",
  "After removing only the production payout registration in a separate scratch copy, all three added tests still passed, but live ingestion rejected the valid payout."
 ]
}

{
 "summary": "No actionable code findings. Both recorded deviations are consistent with the fixed intent. Runtime acceptance remains UNVERIFIED because this checkout has no installed dependencies.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "deviations": [
  {
