{
 "summary": "Found 10 defects. Deterministic Bun probes confirmed duplicate execution, shutdown state corruption, lost retry state, unbounded timeout retries, premature disposal, lost result writes, and stale checkpoints. No project files were changed.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "Claim jobs before awaiting a rate-limit slot",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "scheduler.ts:47-51",
   "Failure": "Two overlapping pollOnce calls can both pass the pending-state and claimed-set checks before either acquire resolves. Both then execute the same job without rechecking. The setInterval callback does not await previous polls. A controlled acquire probe started the same job twice.",
   "Fix": "Reserve each job synchronously before acquire. Release the reservation if acquisition fails or shutdown cancels admission."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Remove abort listeners after every execution attempt",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "scheduler.ts:60-64,72-80",
   "Failure": "The abort listener is removed only when an aborted handler rejects. Successful and ordinarily failed attempts retain their listeners. stop() therefore changes completed jobs back to pending. For transient retries, the listener saves the obsolete job object over the newer retry object. Probes changed done to pending and reset a scheduled retry from attempt 1 to attempt 0, also discarding its backoff deadline.",
   "Fix": "Always remove the attempt's abort listener in finally before releasing ownership. Only active attempts may requeue their jobs."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Prevent waiting poll passes from starting jobs after shutdown",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "scheduler.ts:49-54",
   "Failure": "stop() checks and cancels neither pending acquisitions nor their continuations. A poll suspended in acquire resumes after stop(), marks the job running, and invokes its handler with an already-aborted signal. Adding an abort listener then does not replay the abort. A probe confirmed a handler started after stop returned and its result was not recorded.",
   "Fix": "Coordinate pending admissions with shutdown. After acquisition, check shutdown before execution and release the acquired slot without starting the job."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Wait for active handlers before disposing their result buffer",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "scheduler.ts:131-132",
   "Failure": "stop() aborts handlers but does not await their settlement before disposing the buffer. An asynchronous handler can resolve during cancellation or before observing its signal. execute then saves done and calls buffer.add after disposal. That exception is swallowed by the signal.aborted branch, permanently omitting the result. A probe produced state done with zero result records after stop returned.",
   "Fix": "Track execution promises and await their settlement before disposing the buffer. Define how cancellation-racing completions update state and results."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Await in-flight writes even when the pending batch is empty",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "write-buffer.ts:48-51",
   "Failure": "flushNow clears pending before awaiting the sink. During that interval, dispose sees an empty pending array and returns without awaiting inflight. Shutdown can finish while an acknowledged result write is still outstanding. A gated-sink probe showed dispose returning before acknowledgment.",
   "Fix": "Always await the tracked in-flight write during disposal, regardless of pending length, then drain any remaining batch."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Preserve failed batches and recover the flush chain",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "write-buffer.ts:23,31-37",
   "Failure": "A rejected sink write loses the batch because pending was cleared first. Its rejected promise remains in inflight, so every later flush fails at await this.inflight before writing anything. Timer-triggered rejections are also unhandled. A probe lost the first batch, left the second pending, invoked the sink only once, and observed two unhandled rejections.",
   "Fix": "Retain ownership of each batch until acknowledgment. On rejection, preserve its records, clear or recover the serialization state, and propagate the failure through an owned asynchronous path."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Count timeouts against the retry limit",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "scheduler.ts:86-90",
   "Failure": "Every JobTimeoutError resets attempt to zero and always uses backoffMs(1). Repeated timeouts therefore bypass MAX_ATTEMPTS indefinitely and never increase their delay. A job starting at attempt 4 was requeued at attempt 0 in the probe. This also contradicts Job.attempt's completed-attempt contract.",
   "Fix": "Increment the attempt for timeouts and apply the same bounded retry and exponential-backoff policy as other transient failures."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Flush the final checkpoint during shutdown",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "scheduler.ts:127-132",
   "Failure": "stop() cancels a pending checkpoint without writing its latest state or awaiting any checkpoint already being saved. A completed-job probe stopped within the debounce window and produced no checkpoint. Active handlers can additionally call markDirty from finally after stop has cleared the timer, creating checkpoint work after shutdown.",
   "Fix": "After stopping admissions and settling executions, cancel debounce scheduling and await a final checkpoint. Prevent markDirty from scheduling further work after shutdown."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Serialize asynchronous checkpoint saves",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "scheduler.ts:113-115",
   "Failure": "The timer clears checkpointTimer and fires save without tracking its promise. A subsequent transition can start another save while the first remains outstanding. A controlled checkpoint store committed the newer empty claimed set first, then overwrote it with the older snapshot containing an already-completed job.",
   "Fix": "Serialize checkpoint writes and coalesce dirty state while a save is outstanding, so older snapshots cannot commit after newer snapshots."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Implement the documented full-jitter distribution",
   "Severity": "P3",
   "Location": "backoff.ts:4-8",
   "Failure": "The function claims full jitter but samples only the upper half of the exponential ceiling. At attempt 1, random values 0, 0.5, and 0.999 produced 500, 750, and 999 milliseconds. This is equal jitter, not full jitter, and prevents delays below half the ceiling.",
   "Fix": "For full jitter, sample from zero to the ceiling. If equal jitter is intended, explicitly document that policy instead, including the first-retry interval."
  }
 ]
}

